<Project>
  <!-- Shared shape for every *.Codegen project: the codegen-only twin of a
       contract assembly.

       Reinforced.Typings drives codegen by reading [TsInterface]/[TsEnum] out
       of an assembly's metadata, so those attributes must be compiled into
       something. For a long time that something was the SHIPPED contract
       assemblies, which meant each of them carried a hard metadata reference
       to Reinforced.Typings.dll while being deliberately deployed without it.
       Anything that then asked a contract type for its attributes threw
       FileNotFoundException, and Enum.ToString() asks, because enum formatting
       looks for [Flags]. A plain mode.ToString() in a net10.0 test threw, and
       so did xunit's rendering of the resulting failure, so the error reported
       itself as its own cause. Nine contract projects had the same leak.

       PrivateAssets="all" did not prevent it and could not: it governs what
       NuGet propagates to referencing projects, not what the compiler bakes
       into the assembly being built.

       A twin recompiles the SAME sources with SITREP_CODEGEN defined, which is
       the only configuration in which the RT attributes and the RtConfig
       classes exist at all. rtcli reads the twin (see codegen.sh); nothing
       references a twin and nothing ships one.

       Each twin sets CodegenTwinSource to the contract project's directory and
       AssemblyName to the SHIPPED assembly's name. Keeping the assembly name
       identical matters: rtcli sees the same assembly identity, type names and
       member order it always did, so the generated SDK stays byte-identical.

       Attributes cannot be moved to a partial declaration instead: C# enums
       cannot be partial, and the enums are exactly the broken case. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>netstandard2.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);SITREP_CODEGEN</DefineConstants>
    <EnableDefaultCompileItems>false</EnableDefaultCompileItems>
    <!-- A netstandard2.0 library does not copy its package assemblies to
         output by default, which would leave Reinforced.Typings.dll absent
         beside the very assembly rtcli loads for its attributes. rtcli carries
         its own copy in the NuGet tools folder, but relying on that is how the
         previous arrangement ended up hand-copying a DLL into a bin directory
         (and leaving it there: on 2026-08-20 a stray copy flowed 29 projects
         deep and hid a dispatch bug for a month). Copy it properly instead. -->
    <CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies>true</CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="$(CodegenTwinSource)\**\*.cs"
             Exclude="$(CodegenTwinSource)\bin\**\*.cs;$(CodegenTwinSource)\obj\**\*.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- A plain reference on purpose: a twin is codegen-only, so
         Reinforced.Typings.dll landing in its output is exactly what we want. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Reinforced.Typings" Version="1.6.7" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
